
In this file, we will learn about how to use x64dbg for exe, then gdb for linux

I. x64dbg
Essential Keyboard Shortcuts
KeyAction
F9  Run / Continue
F8  Step Over (next line)
F7  Step Into (enter function)
F4  Run to cursor
F2  Toggle breakpoint
Ctrl+G  Go to address
Ctrl+F  Search in disassembly
Space  Assemble / patch instruction
Ctrl+B  Search for byte pattern

Setting Breakpoints
Software Breakpoint (most common)

Click on a line → press F2
// Red highlight = breakpoint set

Breakpoint on API call
In the Command Bar (bottom):
bp CreateFileA
bp VirtualAlloc
bp WriteProcessMemory

Hardware Breakpoint (on memory access)
Right-click address in dump →
Breakpoint → Hardware → On Access/Write/Execute
Conditional Breakpoint
Right-click a BP → Edit →
Set condition: EAX == 1
// Only pauses when condition is true

Navigating Disassembly
Ctrl+G          → jump to any address or symbol
                   e.g: 0x401000  or  MessageBoxA

Right-click → Follow in Disassembler   → jump to call target
Right-click → Follow in Memory Dump    → inspect raw bytes

; To go back after jumping:
Alt+Left Arrow  → navigate back

Reading Registers Panel
EIP / RIP  →  current instruction pointer (where you are)
ESP / RSP  →  stack pointer
EAX / RAX  →  usually holds return values
EBX, ECX, EDX  →  general purpose

Flags:
ZF (Zero Flag)   →  set when result = 0
CF (Carry Flag)  →  arithmetic carry
SF (Sign Flag)   →  negative result

Double-click any register to modify its value


Patching Instructions
NOP out a jump (bypass check)
1. Click the JNZ / JE instruction
2. Press Space → type: nop
3. Repeat for each byte if needed

Or right-click → Patch → Fill with NOPs
Save patched binary
File → Patch File → select all patches → Patch File
→ saves a new fixed .exe

Searching for Strings & Patterns
Find hardcoded strings
Right-click in disassembly →
Search For → All Referenced Strings
→ double-click any string to jump to it
Find all API calls
Right-click → Search For → All Intermodular Calls
Search bytes (pattern scan)
Ctrl+B → enter hex pattern: FF 25 ?? ?? ?? ??

Memory Dump Panel
Ctrl+G in dump  → go to any address
Right-click     → Follow in Disassembler
                → Set Breakpoint
                → Modify bytes directly

; To watch a variable:
Right-click register (e.g. EAX) →
"Follow in Dump" → tracks memory it points to
